  "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
